1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:02,970
In this video, we'll
continue our discussion of

2
00:00:02,970 --> 00:00:05,400
Linux device update
and talk specifically

3
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:08,820
about how we might architect
the device updates solution

4
00:00:08,820 --> 00:00:13,020
for Linux device update
of the root filesystem.

5
00:00:13,020 --> 00:00:15,030
We want to understand

6
00:00:15,030 --> 00:00:17,160
some embedded Linux device
updates strategy and

7
00:00:17,160 --> 00:00:19,770
tradeoffs and
introduce a couple of

8
00:00:19,770 --> 00:00:21,090
open source frameworks that you

9
00:00:21,090 --> 00:00:23,430
can use for device update.

10
00:00:23,780 --> 00:00:27,210
The first example I want to talk

11
00:00:27,210 --> 00:00:30,015
about is a way you can update

12
00:00:30,015 --> 00:00:32,820
Linux-based device using
what's called an A/B

13
00:00:32,820 --> 00:00:34,890
or you might hear this

14
00:00:34,890 --> 00:00:38,070
discussed as a dual
redundant update scheme.

15
00:00:38,070 --> 00:00:40,165
If we start with the bootloader,

16
00:00:40,165 --> 00:00:41,210
that's going to take a side of

17
00:00:41,210 --> 00:00:42,710
the initial reset factor and get

18
00:00:42,710 --> 00:00:44,735
us to the point that
we're loading images.

19
00:00:44,735 --> 00:00:46,130
The point where we decide

20
00:00:46,130 --> 00:00:48,350
which Linux image and

21
00:00:48,350 --> 00:00:51,805
which root filesystem
we're going to boot from,

22
00:00:51,805 --> 00:00:54,380
we can have some logic

23
00:00:54,380 --> 00:00:56,240
in the bootloader
that can help us pick

24
00:00:56,240 --> 00:00:59,360
which of these root filesystems

25
00:00:59,360 --> 00:01:00,665
that we want to boot from.

26
00:01:00,665 --> 00:01:03,185
We can have two root
filesystems that we support

27
00:01:03,185 --> 00:01:06,185
as a part of our
filesystem image.

28
00:01:06,185 --> 00:01:08,120
Then when we're running out of

29
00:01:08,120 --> 00:01:09,995
one of the two root filesystems,

30
00:01:09,995 --> 00:01:15,050
if we have a request come
in to update the system,

31
00:01:15,050 --> 00:01:17,435
we can start writing
that new update payload

32
00:01:17,435 --> 00:01:19,750
into a new root
filesystem partition,

33
00:01:19,750 --> 00:01:22,665
that's our carrier
inactive partition.

34
00:01:22,665 --> 00:01:24,710
If something would
happen and we were

35
00:01:24,710 --> 00:01:27,145
interrupted while we're
going through that process,

36
00:01:27,145 --> 00:01:28,505
it wouldn't matter because

37
00:01:28,505 --> 00:01:30,710
the current boot partition
is the one that we're

38
00:01:30,710 --> 00:01:32,810
currently using and it

39
00:01:32,810 --> 00:01:35,665
won't ever be corrupted
with this scenario.

40
00:01:35,665 --> 00:01:37,685
As part of the step,

41
00:01:37,685 --> 00:01:40,430
if you start writing the
new update payload and then

42
00:01:40,430 --> 00:01:43,685
you complete that
process successfully,

43
00:01:43,685 --> 00:01:47,105
you could at that point
inform the bootloader that

44
00:01:47,105 --> 00:01:51,560
the second partition is
now available to be used,

45
00:01:51,560 --> 00:01:53,330
and tell the bootloader that on

46
00:01:53,330 --> 00:01:56,035
the next boot to use
this new partition.

47
00:01:56,035 --> 00:01:57,860
Then on the next
boot, the bootloader

48
00:01:57,860 --> 00:01:59,315
will go ahead and switch

49
00:01:59,315 --> 00:02:02,120
the partition A to inactive

50
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:06,090
and start booting from
partition B instead.

51
00:02:06,470 --> 00:02:10,310
Then you can see this whole
process would be repeated in

52
00:02:10,310 --> 00:02:11,660
the case that there was

53
00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:13,640
a new update that happened
from partition B,

54
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:16,400
you just write over partition A,

55
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:18,770
tell the bootloader about
the new back boot partition

56
00:02:18,770 --> 00:02:19,940
and then the next boot would

57
00:02:19,940 --> 00:02:21,455
boot back from
partition A again.

58
00:02:21,455 --> 00:02:23,000
You can just keep going back and

59
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,010
forth between these
two partitions.

60
00:02:25,010 --> 00:02:27,590
You can also build
some redundancy logic

61
00:02:27,590 --> 00:02:28,910
into your bootloader to say,

62
00:02:28,910 --> 00:02:33,380
"If I don't boot far enough
into the new partition,

63
00:02:33,380 --> 00:02:34,550
then maybe that means there's

64
00:02:34,550 --> 00:02:36,095
some problem with the update,

65
00:02:36,095 --> 00:02:38,510
and I want to fail-safe and
enroll back to what I know is

66
00:02:38,510 --> 00:02:41,900
working before in the
previous partition."

67
00:02:42,370 --> 00:02:45,300
If the boot fails,

68
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:47,810
the bootloader can say, "I
know I was able to boot

69
00:02:47,810 --> 00:02:49,790
successfully to the last image,

70
00:02:49,790 --> 00:02:51,785
so I'll just go ahead
and roll back to that,

71
00:02:51,785 --> 00:02:54,050
wait for another instruction

72
00:02:54,050 --> 00:02:57,195
of how to boot to
the new image."

73
00:02:57,195 --> 00:02:59,585
This can be a really
nice feature to have

74
00:02:59,585 --> 00:03:01,490
with a headless embedded
system, for instance,

75
00:03:01,490 --> 00:03:03,350
where it's absolutely critical

76
00:03:03,350 --> 00:03:04,610
that the device be able to boot

77
00:03:04,610 --> 00:03:05,990
up and be able to
communicate with

78
00:03:05,990 --> 00:03:08,320
the server or else you're
going to end up to the break.

79
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:10,850
Having this extra
layer of protection,

80
00:03:10,850 --> 00:03:13,340
to know that we booted
back up successfully in

81
00:03:13,340 --> 00:03:16,370
the redundant scheme
can mean that we

82
00:03:16,370 --> 00:03:17,720
can fully fall back to

83
00:03:17,720 --> 00:03:20,520
whatever was working
before as needed.

84
00:03:23,930 --> 00:03:26,330
One thing to think about
when we're talking

85
00:03:26,330 --> 00:03:28,115
about a dual redundant scheme,

86
00:03:28,115 --> 00:03:29,960
if we're booting from

87
00:03:29,960 --> 00:03:33,650
these two different root
filesystem partitions,

88
00:03:33,650 --> 00:03:36,050
we're going to essentially
be swapping out

89
00:03:36,050 --> 00:03:38,945
the entire root filesystem
with each boot,

90
00:03:38,945 --> 00:03:41,840
and so pretty much

91
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:43,700
every device you will
work with that'll

92
00:03:43,700 --> 00:03:45,785
some amount of
configuration that's

93
00:03:45,785 --> 00:03:48,190
customizable for a
given application.

94
00:03:48,190 --> 00:03:49,460
If you think about,
for instance,

95
00:03:49,460 --> 00:03:51,245
a network connected device,

96
00:03:51,245 --> 00:03:52,940
most of the time the user is

97
00:03:52,940 --> 00:03:54,620
going to need to
enter something like

98
00:03:54,620 --> 00:03:57,380
which wireless access point

99
00:03:57,380 --> 00:03:58,580
you're going to be connected to,

100
00:03:58,580 --> 00:04:00,050
and maybe something
about the password

101
00:04:00,050 --> 00:04:02,035
for that wireless access point.

102
00:04:02,035 --> 00:04:03,570
If we're going to be completely

103
00:04:03,570 --> 00:04:05,245
replacing the root filesystem,

104
00:04:05,245 --> 00:04:06,650
then obviously we won't be able

105
00:04:06,650 --> 00:04:08,340
to place that in the
root filesystem,

106
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:10,130
or when we boot it to
the new root filesystem

107
00:04:10,130 --> 00:04:12,940
that data would not
be available anymore.

108
00:04:12,940 --> 00:04:15,440
In order to handle
configuration data that's

109
00:04:15,440 --> 00:04:17,180
modified on the root filesystem,

110
00:04:17,180 --> 00:04:19,520
you do that with a
separate data partition.

111
00:04:19,520 --> 00:04:22,445
Usually when you talk about
an A/B dual redundant scheme,

112
00:04:22,445 --> 00:04:24,455
you'll also have a separate
data partition that

113
00:04:24,455 --> 00:04:27,535
includes anything that's
persistent about the product.

114
00:04:27,535 --> 00:04:31,080
That's where you could
place all the modifications

115
00:04:31,080 --> 00:04:34,430
that you'd need to

116
00:04:34,430 --> 00:04:36,125
keep across these
different update

117
00:04:36,125 --> 00:04:38,780
and different root
filesystem images.

118
00:04:39,380 --> 00:04:44,505
In addition to the
dual A/B scheme,

119
00:04:44,505 --> 00:04:46,730
then you can also consider doing

120
00:04:46,730 --> 00:04:49,895
a single copy scheme where

121
00:04:49,895 --> 00:04:50,930
instead of booting to

122
00:04:50,930 --> 00:04:53,719
two different complete root
filesystem partitions,

123
00:04:53,719 --> 00:04:56,210
you might instead have
an [inaudible] or

124
00:04:56,210 --> 00:04:58,760
some dedicated update
support partition

125
00:04:58,760 --> 00:05:02,030
that maybe doesn't have all of

126
00:05:02,030 --> 00:05:05,180
the logic that's in
the root filesystem,

127
00:05:05,180 --> 00:05:07,520
but has just enough
to detect whether

128
00:05:07,520 --> 00:05:10,010
the root filesystem
partition is bootable,

129
00:05:10,010 --> 00:05:11,810
so whether an update
actually completed on

130
00:05:11,810 --> 00:05:15,435
the partition and also
to support updates.

131
00:05:15,435 --> 00:05:18,290
In the case that we
weren't able to complete

132
00:05:18,290 --> 00:05:20,900
an update previously
for whatever reason,

133
00:05:20,900 --> 00:05:22,520
the logic would
need to be here to

134
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:24,815
actually support a new update,

135
00:05:24,815 --> 00:05:28,130
and sometimes this could
be more limited like

136
00:05:28,130 --> 00:05:31,100
maybe instead of
being able to support

137
00:05:31,100 --> 00:05:34,430
an update on Bluetooth
or Wi-Fi connectivity,

138
00:05:34,430 --> 00:05:35,600
maybe you could only support

139
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:38,185
an update of a Bluetooth
or something like that.

140
00:05:38,185 --> 00:05:41,750
It can tradeoff some

141
00:05:41,750 --> 00:05:45,245
of the storage space
requirements of having

142
00:05:45,245 --> 00:05:50,735
a complete dual redundant root
filesystem partition with

143
00:05:50,735 --> 00:05:53,330
the ability to still be
able to recover from

144
00:05:53,330 --> 00:05:55,010
cases where we weren't able to

145
00:05:55,010 --> 00:05:57,425
complete the previous
update successfully.

146
00:05:57,425 --> 00:05:59,540
In many cases, you'll
still end up with

147
00:05:59,540 --> 00:06:01,640
a separate Data
Partition containing

148
00:06:01,640 --> 00:06:04,280
the customizations that
would happen as a part

149
00:06:04,280 --> 00:06:07,235
of the root file system when
the user's operating it.

150
00:06:07,235 --> 00:06:08,600
Because again, you
don't want to make your

151
00:06:08,600 --> 00:06:10,160
update- replace

152
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:13,890
that content that was
customized by the user.

153
00:06:14,290 --> 00:06:16,895
In addition to
those two schemes,

154
00:06:16,895 --> 00:06:19,040
there's a third
option where instead

155
00:06:19,040 --> 00:06:21,305
of updating the entire
root file system,

156
00:06:21,305 --> 00:06:24,440
maybe you just update
specific applications.

157
00:06:24,440 --> 00:06:27,230
If you think about the way
this works, for instance,

158
00:06:27,230 --> 00:06:29,270
with your Ubuntu system

159
00:06:29,270 --> 00:06:30,845
that you're using
for development.

160
00:06:30,845 --> 00:06:32,975
Even if you do a
full system update,

161
00:06:32,975 --> 00:06:34,370
what you're really
doing is updating

162
00:06:34,370 --> 00:06:37,010
a whole bunch of
applications individually.

163
00:06:37,010 --> 00:06:39,380
The individual applications
can be updated with

164
00:06:39,380 --> 00:06:42,110
packages like.deb and.rpm,

165
00:06:42,110 --> 00:06:44,120
and using apt for instance,

166
00:06:44,120 --> 00:06:46,700
where we use for it
with Ubuntu or yum to

167
00:06:46,700 --> 00:06:50,480
handle upgrades of each one
of these individual packages.

168
00:06:50,480 --> 00:06:54,185
The upgrades might include
application binaries,

169
00:06:54,185 --> 00:06:58,685
start and run scripts,
configuration files, etc.

170
00:06:58,685 --> 00:07:01,460
If you are using yocto,

171
00:07:01,460 --> 00:07:03,830
yocto does build update packages

172
00:07:03,830 --> 00:07:06,710
as a part of the full
yocto image build.

173
00:07:06,710 --> 00:07:08,810
This is something that is in

174
00:07:08,810 --> 00:07:11,420
some ways natively
supported by yocto.

175
00:07:11,420 --> 00:07:13,430
It's also the standard
scheme that you'd

176
00:07:13,430 --> 00:07:15,380
use for Linux
distribution updates.

177
00:07:15,380 --> 00:07:18,830
Again, this is what we've been
using for Ubuntu already.

178
00:07:18,830 --> 00:07:21,455
Some trade-offs. This is, again,

179
00:07:21,455 --> 00:07:24,590
a case where there's not really
a right or wrong answer.

180
00:07:24,590 --> 00:07:26,300
There's a lot of trade-offs
you need to be aware

181
00:07:26,300 --> 00:07:28,220
of with these different
updates schemes.

182
00:07:28,220 --> 00:07:29,690
You need to pick the right one

183
00:07:29,690 --> 00:07:32,210
for whatever your
application is.

184
00:07:32,210 --> 00:07:35,899
Talking first about
A/B update benefits,

185
00:07:35,899 --> 00:07:38,300
it's really nice to being
able to completely roll

186
00:07:38,300 --> 00:07:40,745
back to a previously working
and shipped release,

187
00:07:40,745 --> 00:07:41,780
and even nicer,

188
00:07:41,780 --> 00:07:43,820
if you can do that
automatically.

189
00:07:43,820 --> 00:07:45,650
If you can detect the fact that

190
00:07:45,650 --> 00:07:47,240
the boot didn't
succeed or complete

191
00:07:47,240 --> 00:07:48,530
successfully and just go back to

192
00:07:48,530 --> 00:07:50,195
whatever worked previously,

193
00:07:50,195 --> 00:07:51,920
that's nice to have that option.

194
00:07:51,920 --> 00:07:54,080
That's one nice thing about A/B.

195
00:07:54,080 --> 00:07:55,910
It's also nice that
you don't have to

196
00:07:55,910 --> 00:07:57,650
worry about partial upgrades or

197
00:07:57,650 --> 00:07:59,330
different application
updates states

198
00:07:59,330 --> 00:08:01,520
getting through different
parts of the process.

199
00:08:01,520 --> 00:08:03,320
It's always either you
always made it all

200
00:08:03,320 --> 00:08:04,940
the way through and
now you're booting

201
00:08:04,940 --> 00:08:08,195
from that image or something
started a partial right,

202
00:08:08,195 --> 00:08:10,370
and you don't really care
because you never got to

203
00:08:10,370 --> 00:08:11,675
the point of switching to that

204
00:08:11,675 --> 00:08:14,030
as your new root file system.

205
00:08:14,030 --> 00:08:17,090
There's a logical
separation then between

206
00:08:17,090 --> 00:08:19,550
the configuration data
and update image.

207
00:08:19,550 --> 00:08:23,720
In other words, all your
configuration data is stored

208
00:08:23,720 --> 00:08:28,020
outside the root file system
and in some data partition,

209
00:08:28,020 --> 00:08:29,440
which makes it much easier to

210
00:08:29,440 --> 00:08:31,000
handle things like
factory reset,

211
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:33,190
because you can just
delete the contents of

212
00:08:33,190 --> 00:08:34,900
the data partition and go back

213
00:08:34,900 --> 00:08:37,545
to the default
state of the image.

214
00:08:37,545 --> 00:08:40,820
Some of the drawbacks of
the A/B update scheme

215
00:08:40,820 --> 00:08:42,620
are that it requires
double the size

216
00:08:42,620 --> 00:08:44,465
of the storage for
the root file system.

217
00:08:44,465 --> 00:08:46,190
Depending on how much
storage you have,

218
00:08:46,190 --> 00:08:48,065
that may or may not be an issue.

219
00:08:48,065 --> 00:08:50,750
Oftentimes, because
flash storage is getting

220
00:08:50,750 --> 00:08:54,350
so cheap and raw file systems
are comparatively small,

221
00:08:54,350 --> 00:08:57,980
this is probably less of
an issue for most devices.

222
00:08:57,980 --> 00:09:03,035
It also requires the full
update size for each image.

223
00:09:03,035 --> 00:09:04,610
In other words,
you're going to be

224
00:09:04,610 --> 00:09:07,160
transferring over the
wire, in most cases,

225
00:09:07,160 --> 00:09:09,680
the full contents of the
root file system partition,

226
00:09:09,680 --> 00:09:11,810
even if it was a
relatively small amount of

227
00:09:11,810 --> 00:09:14,600
change that you made between
two different releases.

228
00:09:14,600 --> 00:09:16,400
There are exceptions to that.

229
00:09:16,400 --> 00:09:18,380
There are delta update
schemes that can help

230
00:09:18,380 --> 00:09:21,830
you just send the
changes over the wire.

231
00:09:21,830 --> 00:09:23,750
But they become a
little bit more

232
00:09:23,750 --> 00:09:26,990
difficult to do with
A/B updates schemes.

233
00:09:26,990 --> 00:09:30,650
That's mostly important if
you're talking about using

234
00:09:30,650 --> 00:09:34,535
something like a low
bandwidth connection,

235
00:09:34,535 --> 00:09:36,800
especially if you're using
something like Roller to

236
00:09:36,800 --> 00:09:39,230
do updates or if you were

237
00:09:39,230 --> 00:09:42,935
using something like LTE or

238
00:09:42,935 --> 00:09:44,360
a connection that is

239
00:09:44,360 --> 00:09:47,760
expensive to transfer
large files over.

240
00:09:47,800 --> 00:09:51,724
The application update scheme,

241
00:09:51,724 --> 00:09:53,390
then the benefit
would be that you can

242
00:09:53,390 --> 00:09:54,845
have smaller updates sizes

243
00:09:54,845 --> 00:09:57,050
and you don't need to have

244
00:09:57,050 --> 00:09:59,480
this second copy of
the file system,

245
00:09:59,480 --> 00:10:02,525
so no additional storage
space really required.

246
00:10:02,525 --> 00:10:04,040
But the drawbacks is

247
00:10:04,040 --> 00:10:06,755
that it's much more
difficult to do rollback.

248
00:10:06,755 --> 00:10:07,820
You have to think
through what would

249
00:10:07,820 --> 00:10:08,900
happen if I'm going to

250
00:10:08,900 --> 00:10:11,915
update 50 applications as a
part of my update process,

251
00:10:11,915 --> 00:10:13,460
and only the first 25

252
00:10:13,460 --> 00:10:15,995
succeeded and the rest of
them failed for some reason.

253
00:10:15,995 --> 00:10:17,870
How do you recover
from that scenario

254
00:10:17,870 --> 00:10:20,945
becomes a pretty
complicated task.

255
00:10:20,945 --> 00:10:22,895
You also might need
to think about,

256
00:10:22,895 --> 00:10:24,815
if I started an update and I got

257
00:10:24,815 --> 00:10:27,170
half the way through
and then I lost power,

258
00:10:27,170 --> 00:10:29,000
how do I recover
from that state?

259
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:30,440
Those two problems are

260
00:10:30,440 --> 00:10:31,940
something you need to
think through and have

261
00:10:31,940 --> 00:10:33,710
a strategy to deal
with if you're going

262
00:10:33,710 --> 00:10:36,480
to go with an application
update scheme.

263
00:10:38,920 --> 00:10:42,170
I like to put together
comparisons like this

264
00:10:42,170 --> 00:10:43,430
when I'm looking at
different options

265
00:10:43,430 --> 00:10:44,795
so that I can understand,

266
00:10:44,795 --> 00:10:47,465
depending on which criteria
I care the most about,

267
00:10:47,465 --> 00:10:50,165
which one might be
the best solution.

268
00:10:50,165 --> 00:10:53,090
Again, looking at storage
size requirements space,

269
00:10:53,090 --> 00:10:54,950
the A/B dual redundant
is going to be

270
00:10:54,950 --> 00:10:56,840
the most storage
space requirement

271
00:10:56,840 --> 00:10:58,820
because you need two
times partition size.

272
00:10:58,820 --> 00:11:00,410
Again, depending on
your application

273
00:11:00,410 --> 00:11:01,670
and the size of
your file system,

274
00:11:01,670 --> 00:11:03,890
this might actually not
be that big of a problem.

275
00:11:03,890 --> 00:11:05,825
But if it is, you need
to be aware of that.

276
00:11:05,825 --> 00:11:08,120
The nice thing about the
single copy is you only

277
00:11:08,120 --> 00:11:11,915
need smaller amount of storage
for upgrade components.

278
00:11:11,915 --> 00:11:13,670
Then if that's really an issue,

279
00:11:13,670 --> 00:11:15,830
this is where application
update might be a good choice

280
00:11:15,830 --> 00:11:18,890
because you really don't
need any additional space.

281
00:11:18,890 --> 00:11:22,295
Talking about redundancy
and rollback capabilities,

282
00:11:22,295 --> 00:11:24,845
AB dual redundant is
the best of all because

283
00:11:24,845 --> 00:11:28,520
every component that you're
updating can be rolled back.

284
00:11:28,520 --> 00:11:30,695
In the single copy case,

285
00:11:30,695 --> 00:11:32,480
most of the components
and the most

286
00:11:32,480 --> 00:11:34,955
important components
specifically the ones

287
00:11:34,955 --> 00:11:37,490
about update,

288
00:11:37,490 --> 00:11:41,450
are always going to be available
in a rollback scenario.

289
00:11:41,450 --> 00:11:43,834
However, it may not be possible

290
00:11:43,834 --> 00:11:46,580
or maybe difficult to
update those components.

291
00:11:46,580 --> 00:11:49,040
If you want to update your
updater, for instance,

292
00:11:49,040 --> 00:11:53,030
single copy becomes more of
a challenge to pull off.

293
00:11:53,030 --> 00:11:55,759
It definitely has some downsides

294
00:11:55,759 --> 00:11:57,815
in terms of redundancy
and rollback.

295
00:11:57,815 --> 00:12:00,230
Then application for
the reasons that we

296
00:12:00,230 --> 00:12:02,375
discussed previously
is the most limited

297
00:12:02,375 --> 00:12:04,910
because rollbacks going to
be pretty complicated and

298
00:12:04,910 --> 00:12:08,280
scenarios that you got
partially through an update.

299
00:12:08,350 --> 00:12:11,405
Then talking about overall
system complexity,

300
00:12:11,405 --> 00:12:14,690
how complex is it to build
and put the system together?

301
00:12:14,690 --> 00:12:17,090
It's probably a
little bit higher for

302
00:12:17,090 --> 00:12:20,285
either the dual redundant
or single copy.

303
00:12:20,285 --> 00:12:21,440
It might get a
little bit into the

304
00:12:21,440 --> 00:12:22,790
specifics about how you do it,

305
00:12:22,790 --> 00:12:23,840
but just given

306
00:12:23,840 --> 00:12:26,330
an extra data partition
and bootloader redundancy,

307
00:12:26,330 --> 00:12:28,580
you might have a little bit
more system complexity to set

308
00:12:28,580 --> 00:12:32,390
up from the starting point.

309
00:12:32,390 --> 00:12:35,690
You have additional
complexity here related to

310
00:12:35,690 --> 00:12:39,470
having the separate partition
that handles updates.

311
00:12:39,470 --> 00:12:42,785
There's probably less
system complexity

312
00:12:42,785 --> 00:12:43,880
for application update.

313
00:12:43,880 --> 00:12:45,470
You're basically paying
for the complexity

314
00:12:45,470 --> 00:12:48,780
with how to handle
redundancy and rollback.

315
00:12:49,930 --> 00:12:54,125
Talking about a couple of
Device Update Framework.

316
00:12:54,125 --> 00:12:56,510
A lot of these pieces

317
00:12:56,510 --> 00:12:57,830
that you're going to
use for your device

318
00:12:57,830 --> 00:12:59,570
update implementation on Linux

319
00:12:59,570 --> 00:13:01,715
are common, everybody uses them.

320
00:13:01,715 --> 00:13:04,790
Wouldn't it be great if
we could leverage a bunch

321
00:13:04,790 --> 00:13:08,640
of open source community
work and of course we can.

322
00:13:08,770 --> 00:13:11,090
These are just a
couple of examples,

323
00:13:11,090 --> 00:13:12,860
there are other projects
out there as well,

324
00:13:12,860 --> 00:13:14,840
but I'm more
familiar with these,

325
00:13:14,840 --> 00:13:17,675
so I'll cover two of the ones
that I've used in the past.

326
00:13:17,675 --> 00:13:20,495
First one I'll talk
about is SWUpdate,

327
00:13:20,495 --> 00:13:23,330
and SWUpdate like
a building block

328
00:13:23,330 --> 00:13:24,860
or a tool that you can use for

329
00:13:24,860 --> 00:13:27,575
upgrades without
really enforcing

330
00:13:27,575 --> 00:13:30,815
or putting together a
specific upgrade design.

331
00:13:30,815 --> 00:13:32,659
This one, for instance,

332
00:13:32,659 --> 00:13:36,485
works with AB single copy or
application updates schemes.

333
00:13:36,485 --> 00:13:38,495
You can do whatever
you want with it.

334
00:13:38,495 --> 00:13:40,430
There's maybe a little
bit more setup work,

335
00:13:40,430 --> 00:13:42,080
but it's probably
more versatile and

336
00:13:42,080 --> 00:13:44,555
flexible in terms
of how you use it.

337
00:13:44,555 --> 00:13:48,500
It again supports
builder it or Yocto,

338
00:13:48,500 --> 00:13:49,970
so it could be used with any of

339
00:13:49,970 --> 00:13:52,220
the schemes that we've talked

340
00:13:52,220 --> 00:13:54,995
about for application
development in this class.

341
00:13:54,995 --> 00:13:57,635
It supports the signing
that we've talked about

342
00:13:57,635 --> 00:14:01,775
before as an important part
of securing device updates.

343
00:14:01,775 --> 00:14:04,040
It integrates with
Eclipse hawkbit

344
00:14:04,040 --> 00:14:05,555
for cloud deployment scenarios.

345
00:14:05,555 --> 00:14:08,015
You can have a cloud
server that's hosting

346
00:14:08,015 --> 00:14:11,345
and controlling your
device update process.

347
00:14:11,345 --> 00:14:15,275
Also a pretty cool feature
of SWUpdate is that there's

348
00:14:15,275 --> 00:14:17,300
integrated web
server that you can

349
00:14:17,300 --> 00:14:19,475
use for deploying your images.

350
00:14:19,475 --> 00:14:21,245
If your devices
Internet connected,

351
00:14:21,245 --> 00:14:25,130
you can start SW updated
a mode that brings up

352
00:14:25,130 --> 00:14:27,590
a web server and its own
webpage that you can use for

353
00:14:27,590 --> 00:14:30,350
interacting and passing
an update payloads.

354
00:14:30,350 --> 00:14:31,820
That could be a pretty
handy thing when

355
00:14:31,820 --> 00:14:34,560
you're starting out
with development.

356
00:14:34,600 --> 00:14:38,465
A second device update
framework is called Mender.

357
00:14:38,465 --> 00:14:40,310
This one is specifically built

358
00:14:40,310 --> 00:14:42,590
around Yocto and
it has some layers

359
00:14:42,590 --> 00:14:44,630
that provide u-boot and

360
00:14:44,630 --> 00:14:46,790
other boot loader
